<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Torghut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Torgut</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Torghut are an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> Mongol tribe. In the early 17th century, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kho-orluk">Kho Orluk</a>, many of them migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> across the Kazakh steppe to the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, where they founded the Kalmyk Khanate. In 1771 most of the Volga Torghut, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan">Ubashi Khan</a>, made a disastrous trek back to Dzungaria under the Qing. Torghut communities live today in Kalmykia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torghut.jpg" alt="Torghut" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Oirat_Confederation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Torghut are one of the four major subgroups of the Four Oirats. Their nobles traced their descent to the Keraite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a>, and many Torghuts descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a>. They may once have served as kheshigs, guards of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khans</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, and the Torghut clan first appeared as an Oirat group in the mid-16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the collapse of the Four Oirat Alliance, most of the Torghuts under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kho-orluk">Kho Orluk</a> moved west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region in 1630 and formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>. A few nobles followed Toro Baikhu Gushi Khan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Qinghai Lake</a> and joined the Upper Mongols. In 1698, 500 Torghuts on pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> could not return home and were resettled on the Ejin River by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>, and in 1699 some 15,000 households went back from the Volga to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a>.</p><p>Harsh treatment by Russian governors drove most of the Volga Torghuts to leave en masse on 5 January 1771. Estimates of the group range from 150,000 to 400,000 people, with perhaps six million animals. Beset by raids, thirst and starvation, about 85,000 survivors reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> and settled with the Qing emperor's permission, but the Qing coerced them into giving up nomadic life for farming as a deliberate policy to weaken them. Around 70,000 who stayed behind, according to legend because the Volga had not frozen, became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>.</p><p>Kalmyk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> under the Russian general Mikhail Kutuzov fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army in 1812. In 1906 the Qing placed the New Torghuts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> district; after Mongolian independence in 1911–12 one prince fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, while the rest were joined to Khovd Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Thomas De Quincey wrote a narrative of the flight from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in 1837, and an exhibition in memory of the exodus stands at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> in Chengde. Torghut communities live today in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Kalmykia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Khovd in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Torghut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Yuan Dynasty in 1368 they formed a union of four main groups, usually listed as the Choros, <b>Torghut</b>, Dörbet and Khoshut, and challenged the Northern Yuan khans for leadership of the steppe.</blockquote><a href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 17th century the <b>Torghut</b>, an Oirat Mongol tribe, under their leader Kho Orluk, migrated west from Dzungaria across the Kazakh steppe, reaching the lower Volga by about 1630 and driving out the Nogai Horde.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalmyk-khanate">Kalmyk Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
